[size=5]Apocalypse — Magic of Skyrim — Enderal Conversion[/size]

[i]Enai Siaion's Apocalypse, rebuilt so Enderal will actually load it — and so it stops sounding like Tamriel.[/i]

Apocalypse adds 373 spells. In Enderal, out of the box, you get [b]none of them[/b] — and not for the reason anyone expects. The mod does not load at all. Not "loads but distributes nothing": the engine skips the plugin outright, silently, with no warning and nothing in any log. Every spell, every scroll, every record is simply not in your game.

This conversion fixes that, gives the spells a way into Enderal's economy, removes the parts that cannot work here, and takes the Daedra out of a world that has never heard of them.

[line]

[size=4]⚠ Requirements and install order — read this part[/size]

[list=1]
[*][b][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/1090]Apocalypse — Magic of Skyrim by Enai Siaion[/url][/b], version [b]10.2.3[/b] exactly. Install it first and normally. Its two BSAs supply every mesh, texture, sound and script — this download contains none of them.
[*]Install this and [b]LET IT OVERWRITE[/b]. It replaces [font=Courier New]Apocalypse - Magic of Skyrim.esp[/font] with a converted build of the same plugin.
[*]Keep [font=Courier New]Apocalypse - Magic of Skyrim.esp[/font] [b]enabled[/b], exactly where it already sits in your load order.
[*]If you used an earlier build of this mod, [b]delete [font=Courier New]Zenderal - Apocalypse.esp[/font][/b]. It no longer exists and will crash the game if left enabled.
[/list]

[b]Do not use this with any other version of Apocalypse.[/b] It overrides records by ID; a different version moves them.

[line]

[size=4]Why a replacement and not a patch[/size]

[b]Enderal runs Skyrim SE 1.5.97, and that engine refuses to load any plugin whose header form version is 1.71.[/b] It does not warn you. It does not log it. The plugin is just absent. Apocalypse is 1.71.

You can watch it happen: with Apocalypse installed and enabled, open the console and type [font=Courier New]help wither 4[/font]. Apocalypse defines a spell called Wither. You will get nothing back. Change four bytes in the plugin header — 1.71 to 1.70, every other byte identical — and the spell is there.

A patch loaded on top cannot fix this, because a patch has to declare Apocalypse as a master, and the engine has already skipped it. The patch then binds to a master that does not exist and the game crashes during load, about nine seconds in, at the main menu. So the plugin itself has to be rebuilt at form version 1.70 — which is what this is.

Once you are rebuilding the plugin anyway, the rest gets fixed properly rather than worked around:

[list=1]
[*][b]Its entire distribution system targets records Enderal does not have.[/b] Apocalypse runs a quest at game start that copies its tomes, scrolls and staves into [b]54 vanilla Skyrim vendor and loot leveled lists[/b]. Enderal replaced [font=Courier New]Skyrim.esm[/font] wholesale — it is base Enderal, not Bethesda's game — and [b]not one of those 54 lists exists[/b]. Neither do the five College of Winterhold ritual globals it gates part of its distribution on. The quest runs, copies nothing into nothing, and reports success.
[*][b]Its staff crafting is built on Dragonborn content.[/b] All 67 staff recipes are made at the [b]Staff Enchanter[/b] out of [b]Heart Stones[/b]. Enderal has neither. The recipes are deleted, and with them the [font=Courier New]Dragonborn.esm[/font] master.
[*][b]It overwrites one of Enderal's worldspaces.[/b] Apocalypse edits worldspace [font=Courier New]00003C[/font], which in Skyrim is [b]Tamriel[/b] — but in Enderal that ID belongs to [b]MQP01Home[/b], the prologue house. Its edit stamps Tamriel's map grid over it, drops the parent link to Vyn and the no-fast-travel flags, and attaches a region list of five IDs of which four do not exist in Enderal and the fifth is a stone wall.
[/list]

Enderal also has no spell tomes at all — it teaches spells from its own spellbooks, fed by its own leveled lists. So there was nowhere for Apocalypse's tomes to land even in principle until something put them there.

[line]

[size=4]What this patch does[/size]

[b]1. Distribution — the part that makes the mod exist.[/b]

[b]160 spell tomes[/b] and [b]130 scrolls[/b] are added to Enderal's own vendor and loot lists, tiered by spell rank so a novice does not find a master spell at level 3:

[code]
  Apocalypse rank      Enderal tier              Roughly
  Novice   (000)       Trader/Loot A             level 1+
  Apprentice (025)     Trader/Loot A and B       level 5-10+
  Adept    (050)       Trader/Loot C             level 18+
  Expert   (075)       Trader/Loot C and D       level 30+
  Master   (100)       Trader/Loot D             level 40+
[/code]

Scrolls go into Enderal's general scroll loot pool. Buy tomes from mages and traders, find them in the world, exactly as you find Enderal's own.

This is done by [b]adding[/b] a single entry to each of Enderal's nine lists, pointing at this patch's own sublists. Every entry Enderal already had is carried through untouched — nothing of the base game's loot is displaced or replaced.

[b]2. Staff crafting is deleted, and with it the Dragonborn master.[/b]

All 67 recipes are built at the [b]Staff Enchanter[/b] out of [b]Heart Stones[/b], and Enderal has neither. Both the bench keyword and the component are Dragonborn FormIDs that resolve to nothing here, so the recipes could never have appeared in any crafting menu. They are removed outright, which takes 134 of the mod's 138 Dragonborn references with them; the remaining four are repointed. The finished plugin declares [b]no Dragonborn master at all[/b].

The staves themselves remain as dormant records and get no distribution. Enderal's own enchanter is untouched.

[b]3. Fabricate Object drops its Staff Enchanter option.[/b]

The spell that conjures a temporary crafting station offered nine, one of them the Dragonborn Staff Enchanter. It now offers the [b]eight[/b] that exist in Enderal — Arcane Enchanter, Alchemy Lab, Tanning Rack, Workbench, Anvil, Cooking Spit, Grindstone, Smelter — all of them present and working. The menu and the station list are index-matched, so both were trimmed together; picking "Smelter" still gives you a smelter.

[b]3b. The prologue house is put back.[/b]

Apocalypse's edit to worldspace [font=Courier New]00003C[/font] is replaced with Enderal's own [font=Courier New]MQP01Home[/font] record, taken from Forgotten Stories so nothing FS changed is lost. That restores its parent link to Vyn, its map offsets, its [i]small world[/i] and [i]no fast travel[/i] flags, and removes the region list pointing at forms Enderal does not have.

Apocalypse's three utility references inside it are kept — one of its quests and one of its factions still point at them, and dropping them would break both.

[b]4. Fifteen summons removed.[/b]

Enderal has no Daedra and no Dwemer. These are never added to any vendor or loot list:

[list]
[*][b]Dremora[/b] — Churl, Pit Fighter, Champion, Honor Guard, Mentor, Assassin
[*][b]Xivilai[/b] — Sorcerer, Lord
[*][b]Other Daedra[/b] — Weeping Daedra, Lord of Bindings, Six Demon Bag, Conjure Herne, Conjure Kyrkrim
[*][b]Atronach Mark[/b] — summons atronachs onto the marked target
[*][b]Conjure Craftlord[/b] — a Dwemer automaton
[/list]

Everything else in Apocalypse's conjuration school stays: the wraiths, liches, totem spirits, the Battlemage, the Necrowitch, the Deadeye Captain, Bag of Rats. Enderal's own magic already raises the dead and binds spirits — [i]Entropy[/i] is one of its five disciplines — so undead and spirit summoning is entirely at home here. Only the Oblivion natives had to go.

[b]5. Elder Scrolls names replaced.[/b]

See the table below.

[line]

[size=4]The renamed spells[/size]

Every replacement is drawn from Enderal's own written lore — its seven gods, its named scholars, its geography — not invented. Renames are applied to every place the name appears: the tome, the spell, the scroll, the magic effect, the enchantment and the description text.

[b]Gods, the world, and the planes[/b]

